            <p>He didn't open the box, and he didn't drop to one knee. He didn't have a grand, pre-planned speech about joinery or stars, because they had already said everything that mattered over the last year in quiet, sunlit kitchens, dusty workshops, and wet hallways. They had built their foundation out of small, ordinary Sundays, alphabetized spices, and burnt pasta sauces.</p>
